# Both forms on purpose. The trailing slash matches a DIRECTORY only, and in a
# repo with ~18 worktrees it is normal to symlink one lane's node_modules into
# another rather than re-install 500 MB. A symlink is not a directory, so
# `node_modules/` alone does NOT ignore it: 2026-09-24 the consolidation
# worktree showed `?? frontend/node_modules` for a symlink into the foundation
# lane, one `git add -A` away from committing an absolute path that exists on
# no other machine. The bare line below catches the symlink too.
node_modules/
node_modules
dist/
# `.env` on its own matches that exact name and nothing else, so the two lines
# that used to follow it (.env.local, .env.production) were an endless game of
# naming the variants somebody might create. docs/ACCOUNTS-GO-LIVE.md §1 asks
# the owner to put SMTP_PASSWORD in an env file, and the obvious name for it —
# .env.staging, .env.mail, .env.live — was on none of those lines. This repo is
# PUBLIC. So: ignore every .env variant and re-admit the one template that is
# meant to be tracked. Checked with `git check-ignore -v`, not by eye.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.log

# Provider credentials, typed at a hidden prompt by
# backend/scripts/razorpay-sandbox-setup.sh. Ignored by directory and at every
# depth, so neither `git add -A` at the root nor `git add .` from inside
# backend/ can pick one up. The setup script does not trust this line: it runs
# `git check-ignore` on the exact path and refuses to write if the answer is no.
.secrets/

# Per-worktree dev-stack scratch: backend/vite logs, PID files and snapshot
# hashes written by a lane's own run scripts. Never committed on any branch
# (`git log --all -- .devlogs` is empty), and `*.log` above caught only part of
# it — the .pid files and the hashes were still committable, so `git add -A` in
# a lane could land a stale PID in the release repo. Ignored by directory.
# Evidence meant to outlive the lane belongs in docs/, not here.
.devlogs/

# Scratch from the reporting lane's demo stack, and its delivery spool. Neither
# was ignored by anything above — `git check-ignore -v` returned no match — so
# `git add -A` would have committed a PID file and, worse, the spool: a report
# artifact holds the company's takings and is addressed to a named recipient,
# which is not a thing to put in a PUBLIC repo because the send had nowhere else
# to go. Not qa/screens/: those ARE the evidence and this repo tracks them (33
# files from the VC-104 and VC-105 lanes), so ignoring the directory would have
# hidden this lane's proof while keeping its predecessors'.
.demo-run/
.reporting-spool/

# Captured mail. backend/scripts/mail-sink.mjs writes every message it accepts
# as .eml, and those bodies hold live 8-digit sign-in codes until redeemed.
# deploy/e2e-isolated.sh points it under .devlogs/ (ignored above), but the
# script's own default is ./.maildrop, so running the sink by hand from a lane
# root would otherwise leave working credentials one `git add -A` away from a
# PUBLIC repo. Ignored by directory and by extension, at every depth.
.maildrop/
*.eml
